</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Enhancing the ability of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) to interpret sheet music is a crucial step toward building AI musicians. However, current research lacks both evaluation benchmarks and training data for sheet music reasoning. To address this, we propose the idea of synthesizing sheet music problems grounded in music theory, which can serve both as evaluation benchmarks and as training data for reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR). We introduce a data synthesis framework that generates verifiable sheet music questions in both textual and visual modalities, leading to the Synthetic Sheet Music Reasoning Benchmark (SSMR-Bench) and a complementary training set. Evaluation results on SSMR-Bench show the importance of models’ reasoning abilities in interpreting sheet music. At the same time, the poor performance of Gemini 2.5-Pro highlights the challenges that MLLMs still face in interpreting sheet music in a visual format. By leveraging synthetic data for RLVR, Qwen3-8B-Base and Qwen2.5-VL-Instruct achieve improvements on the SSMR-Bench. Besides, the trained Qwen3-8B-Base surpasses GPT-4 in overall performance on MusicTheoryBench and achieves reasoning performance comparable to GPT-4 with the strategies of Role play and Chain-of-Thought. Notably, its performance on math problems also improves relative to the original Qwen3-8B-Base. Furthermore, our results show that the enhanced reasoning ability can also facilitate music composition. In conclusion, we are the first to propose the idea of synthesizing sheet music problems based on music theory rules, and demonstrate its effectiveness not only in advancing model reasoning for sheet music understanding but also in unlocking new possibilities for AI-assisted music creation.
Our code and resources are available at <a href="https://github.com/Linzwcs/AutoMusicTheoryQA" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">GitHub</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"The sheet music is the language of musicians."</span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) and Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have inspired researchers to explore the potential of developing AI musicians <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradshaw &amp; Colton, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Given that sheet music is the universal language of musicians, the ability to read and interpret it is an essential step for AI musicians  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
This requires a grasp of the core concepts of music theory, which provide the structural and expressive framework necessary for musical interpretation. Enhancing LLMs and MLLMs to interpret sheet music involves not just recognizing musical symbols but also modeling the cognitive processes involved in understanding music.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, research on sheet music reasoning remains limited, with a lack of standardized evaluation benchmarks and training datasets. For example, ChatMusician <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduces MusicTheoryBench, a benchmark consisting of 367 human-designed questions, some of which specifically target sheet music understanding in ABC notation, a textual format of sheet music. MMMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite> provides a small set of sheet music understanding questions in image format, though only 30 of them are publicly released with answers. In contrast, MusiXQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> contains a larger set of image-based sheet music data, but it mainly targets the recognition of individual elements rather than reasoning about their connections within music theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by recent human-programmatic approaches to data synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we propose to leverage the rules of music theory to programmatically generate an endless supply of verifiable reasoning problems about sheet music.
Such a resource is invaluable for creating robust evaluation benchmarks and for training models with verifiable rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to improve their reasoning abilities.
The practical implementation of this concept is our novel data synthesis framework. This system is designed to generate questions with staff notation presented in both textual and visual formats and includes nine Question Template Classes, categorized into Rhythm, Chord, Interval, and Scale.
We use this framework to build the Synthetic Sheet Music Reasoning Benchmark (SSMR-Bench). The benchmark includes an evaluation set with 1,600 textual and 1,600 visual QA pairs, and a larger training set with 8,000 pairs for each modality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate SSMR-Bench using a range of state-of-the-art LLMs and MLLMs. For the textual QA task, which represents sheet music in ABC notation, Qwen3-32B demonstrates performance on par with Deepseek-V3, while Deepseek-R1 exceeds Deepseek-V3 by 18.69% overall. This highlights the model’s enhanced reasoning capability in understanding sheet music.
For the visual QA task, Gemini2.5-Pro achieves the best performance with an average score of 56.62%, surpassing Qwen2.5-VL-72B-Instruct, InternVL3-78B, and other models.
However, this performance is significantly lower than when the same questions are presented in textual format, highlighting the challenges of interpreting sheet music from images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We train Qwen3-8B-Base and Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct on our synthetic dataset using Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a></cite>).
Both models achieve substantial improvements. Specifically, the trained Qwen3-8B-Base achieved a 13.06% improvement on our SSMR-Bench over Qwen3-8B, reaching performance comparable to GPT-4.1-mini. Similarly, the trained Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct improves its ability to recognize and analyze sheet music from images, allowing it to outperform Gemini2.5-Pro. To verify the effectiveness of our synthetic data, we further evaluate the trained Qwen3-8B-Base on MusicTheoryBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The results show that the model outperforms GPT-4 in overall performance on MusicTheoryBench and achieves reasoning performance comparable to GPT-4 with Role Play and Chain-of-Thought. Besides, training Qwen3-8B-Base on our synthetic data also boosts its performance on math problems, surpassing the original model’s capabilities.
Furthermore, the improved reasoning ability in sheet music understanding can also facilitate music composition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, we summarize our key contributions as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are the first to propose the idea of leveraging music theory rules to programmatically synthesize verifiable sheet music problems, using them as both an evaluation benchmark and a training set for Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a data synthesis framework based on this idea, which is capable of generating verifiable sheet music questions with staff notation in both textual and visual modalities, and we provide SSMR-Bench along with a corresponding training set in both modalities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct comprehensive experiments to demonstrate the potential of automatically synthesizing verifiable sheet music problems based on music theory rules. Training on this synthetic data enhances models’ reasoning abilities in sheet music, and this improved ability also shows promise in facilitating music composition.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Sheet Music Understanding</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The application of artificial intelligence to sheet music analysis has consistently been a focal point for the research community <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fornés et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shatri &amp; Fazekas, 2024</a>)</cite>. Optical Music Recognition (OMR) employs AI-driven tools to interpret music notation, necessitating both specialized expertise and domain knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tuggener et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Calvo-Zaragoza &amp; Rizo, 2018</a>)</cite>. Some research in this area focuses on the recognition of handwritten music scores, introducing widely used benchmarks and datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Torras et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mayer et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, while other studies propose novel methodologies to address the underlying challenges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rebelo et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rıos-Vila et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ríos-Vila et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While significant progress has been made, some researchers are expanding the study to Sheet Music Question Answering (QA), moving beyond traditional music recognition and image modalities. MMMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite> provides music QA, highlighting the challenges of sheet music QA based on images. Meanwhile, there is growing interest in using LLMs for symbolic music <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, where sheet music is represented in textual formats, such as the widely used ABC notation.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al. (2024)</a></cite> introduces a MusicTheoryBench that includes knowledge and reasoning categories to evaluate the music understanding abilities of LLMs.
However, the manually crafted nature of the two benchmarks hinders data scalability, which is the problem we aim to address in this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Reinforcement Learning Through Synthetic Data</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement Learning (RL) has proven to be a critical factor in enhancing the reasoning capabilities of models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Following the success of DeepSeek-R1, recent work has shifted towards Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR), which utilizes objectively verifiable rewards derived from objective questions and their corresponding answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
For RLVR, how to obtain the verifiable data is a crucial question. Recently, some research has attempted to address this challenge by synthesizing data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Enigmata <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> provides fully synthesizable data across 36 puzzle problems, while SynLogic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> proposes a data synthesis framework that generates diverse logical reasoning data, covering 35 distinct tasks.
Compared to these domains, music is a distinct field, yet it still follows common rules that can be applied to design verifiable questions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perricone, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mulholland &amp; Hojnacki, 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terefenko, 2017</a>)</cite>, which is the core focus of this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Synthesize Music Reasoning Questions</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Data Synthesize Framework</h3>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.04059v1/music_bench.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/201;" width="548" height="201" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Overview of synthesizing verifiable sheet music questions. Experts define rule-based Question Template Classes that generate questions and answers from ABC notation, where the music sheet can be converted into an image, enabling the construction of VQA-style questions. The process is fully programmatic, without LLMs.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Music is traditionally written on a staff, a set of five lines where visual symbols represent the core elements of music theory. The vertical position of a notehead indicates its pitch, while its shape (e.g., hollow or filled, with a stem or flag) defines its rhythm, or duration. These visual cues are organized into measures, separated by barlines, according to a time signature that dictates the underlying rhythmic pulse.
While staff notation is intuitive for human musicians, its graphical nature is cumbersome for programmatic analysis. To bridge this gap, we utilize ABC notation, a text-based format that encodes the same musical information in a machine-readable way. In ABC, pitches are represented by letters (C, D, E), rhythmic values are specified with numerical modifiers, and measures are delineated by vertical bars (|). This format allows us to translate the abstract rules of music theory into parsable strings. Examples of ABC notation are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Dataset Details ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework is designed to generate questions that test a model’s understanding of these fundamental principles, from rhythmic calculation and chord identification to the analysis of intervals and scales.
We outline the process of synthesizing sheet music questions based on music theory rules in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Data Synthesize Framework ‣ 3 Synthesize Music Reasoning Questions ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Sheet music is first obtained in ABC notation. Then, the experts design specialized Question Template Classes derived from music theory rules, each capable of generating both questions and corresponding answers from the ABC representation. Applying these templates produces textual QA pairs. Subsequently, the sheet music in each question can be converted into images using <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">abcm2ps<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">abcm2ps converts ABC notation to PostScript sheet music. </span><a href="https://github.com/lewdlime/abcm2ps" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_upright">https://github.com/lewdlime/abcm2ps</a></span></span></span></span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ImageMagick<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Tool for converting images, e.g., PS or SVG to JPG. </span><a href="https://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_upright">https://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick</a></span></span></span></span>, enabling the construction of VQA questions. The entire pipeline is fully programmatic, without reliance on LLMs, ensuring controllability and interpretability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Data Construction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The scalable synthesis framework we designed currently provides 9 types of questions.
All questions are categorized into four categories: Rhythm, Chord, Interval, and Scale, which are the fundamental elements of sheet music. The cases for each question type are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Question Class Descriptions ‣ Appendix A Dataset Details ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.04059v1/vqa_sunburst.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:220/220;" width="220" height="220"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The composition of the SSMR-Bench test set consists of four categories: Rhythm, Chord, Interval, and Scale. Each category contributes equally, accounting for 25% of the total.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rhythm Questions</span> challenge models to understand the rhythm of sheet music. These questions require models to calculate note values and analyze the music from a holistic perspective.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chord Questions</span> assess models’ reasoning ability to identify and infer chord structures from given notes, testing their understanding of harmonic relationships and tonal context.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interval Questions</span> evaluate the model’s ability to recognize and compute the distance between two notes in terms of pitch, requiring a nuanced understanding of musical intervals.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scale Questions</span> test the model’s proficiency in identifying scales and key signatures, analyzing the relationships between notes and their positions within different scale types.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To solve these questions, LLMs and MLLMs need basic music knowledge, sheet music reading skills, and reasoning ability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Through our data synthesis framework, we introduce the Synthesize Sheet Music Reasoning Benchmark (SSMR-Bench), comprising 1,600 textual and 1,600 visual questions.
The sheet music used to generate questions is sampled from MelodyHub <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, a large-scale dataset in ABC notation.
The distribution of question types and categories within the benchmark is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Data Construction ‣ 3 Synthesize Music Reasoning Questions ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Furthermore, we offer a comprehensive training set, consisting of 8,000 textual and 8,000 visual questions. A comparative analysis of our framework with prior music sheet-related benchmarks is presented in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Data Construction ‣ 3 Synthesize Music Reasoning Questions ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Our framework produces both visual and textual modality questions for music sheet reasoning and constructs a verifiable training set without human curation, thereby enabling scalable data generation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison of music-related QA datasets across different aspects.</span> Checkmarks (✓) denote presence, crosses (✗) absence, and triangles (<math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle</annotation></semantics></math>) partial coverage.</figcaption>
