<figure id="S4.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.04059v1/training_rewards.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/363;" width="548" height="363"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.04059v1/response_length.svg" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/363;" width="548" height="363"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Training dynamics of GRPO on textual and visual modality. (Left) The reward curves for both Qwen3-8B-Base + Textual GRPO and Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct + Visual GRPO show a steady increase, indicating that both models are effectively learning from the reward signal. (Right) The average response length of both models initially decreases and then increases. While the Qwen3-8B-Base model shows a steady increase, the response length of Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct experiences notable fluctuations in the later stages of training.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Results Analysis by Question Type</h3>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.04059v1/question.type.qa.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/142;" width="548" height="142"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.04059v1/question.type.vqa.svg" id="S4.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/148;" width="548" height="148"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance comparison of various selected LLMs and MLLMs on different music-related question types.</span>
The upper plot shows the performance of LLMs evaluated on the textual modality, while the lower plot displays the performance of MLLMs in the visual modality.
The performance is assessed across nine distinct question categories, such as Bar Placement, Chord Identification (Chord ID), and Scale Identification (Scale ID). Detailed descriptions of these question types can be found in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Dataset Details ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To gain deeper insights into the model’s performance, we select various LLMs and MLLMs and present their performance categorized by question type. We present the results in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Results Analysis by Question Type ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The upper plot of Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Results Analysis by Question Type ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the performance of LLMs in the textual modality. After reinforcement learning on 8,000 synthetic music reasoning samples through GRPO, Qwen3-8B-Base outperforms Qwen3-8B in 2/3 of the question types, despite the latter undergoing extensive data training. Additionally, Qwen3-8B-Base+GRPO achieves comparable performance to Deepseek-V3 and Qwen3-32B in aspects such as "Time Sig", "Interval No", and "Note Comp". However, for "Chord ID" and "Scale Sel," the model shows limited improvement, which can be attributed to the scarcity of these data types in the training set shown in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Dataset Details ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The lower plot of Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Results Analysis by Question Type ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the performance of various selected MLLMs in the visual modality. The results highlight the superior performance of the Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct+GRPO model, which consistently achieves the highest accuracy across all question types. This improvement stems from reinforcement learning, which enhances the ability of Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct to accurately recognize elements of sheet music in image format and perform reasoning based on these visual cues.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Beyond Synthetic Data and Question Answering Tasks</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>RL on Synthetic Data Benefits Real-World Scenarios</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the effectiveness of our synthetic data, we conduct experiments on MusicTheoryBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Specifically, we evaluate the performance of Qwen3-8B-Base after GRPO on the synthetic sheet music problems and compare it against three baselines: the unmodified Qwen3-8B-Base, the officially released reasoning model Qwen3-8B, and a GRPO-trained variant using MATH data.
Additionally, we report results for GPT-4, GPT-4 with Role Play, and GPT-4 with Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to provide further context. The results are summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.1 RL on Synthetic Data Benefits Real-World Scenarios ‣ 5 Beyond Synthetic Data and Question Answering Tasks ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Besides, we also evaluate the Qwen3-8B-Base model and its variants on a series of math benchmarks, and present results in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.1 RL on Synthetic Data Benefits Real-World Scenarios ‣ 5 Beyond Synthetic Data and Question Answering Tasks ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.1 RL on Synthetic Data Benefits Real-World Scenarios ‣ 5 Beyond Synthetic Data and Question Answering Tasks ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, Qwen3-8B-Base demonstrates substantial performance gains after GRPO training on the synthetic data. Its original performance was 37.92% in knowledge and 19.39% in reasoning, averaging 28.65%. After GRPO, the model improves by 25.28% in knowledge and 17.34% in reasoning, corresponding to an average gain of 21.32%. Compared to Qwen3-8B, the improvement is mainly observed in reasoning (up by 7.14%) with a marginal increase in knowledge (0.37%). In contrast, the Qwen3-8B-Base variant trained with GRPO on MATH data exhibits much smaller gains, achieving only a 33.81% average score, which is 12.4% lower than Qwen3-8B. Besides, Qwen3-8B-Base reinforced with our synthetic data outperforms GPT-4 in overall performance and achieves reasoning capabilities comparable to GPT-4 with Role Play and GPT-4 with CoT.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the mathematical domain, training the Qwen3-8B-Base model on our synthetic dataset yields substantial improvements. After training, the model demonstrates enhanced accuracy across multiple benchmarks. For example, its performance on the AIME24 dataset increases from 8.75% to 13.75%, while on the AMC dataset, accuracy rises from 39.16% to 52.11%.
The model also demonstrates significant improvements on Minerva, increasing from 25.37% to 34.19%, and on Olympiad Bench, rising from 30.22% to 40.44%. Moreover, when evaluated on the MATH-500 dataset, the Qwen3-8B-Base+Music RL model achieves a remarkable accuracy of 79.40%. Nevertheless, this performance remains significantly lower than that of the version trained on math-specific data, highlighting the critical importance of domain-specific datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This confirms the effectiveness of synthesizing verifiable questions specifically related to sheet music in enhancing the model’s understanding and reasoning abilities within this domain, while yielding only moderate gains in Math problem-solving. In contrast, applying GRPO to MATH data yields substantially smaller gains in sheet music reasoning while producing significant improvements in mathematical tasks, underscoring the critical importance of domain relevance in synthetic data generation. Overall, these findings suggest that automatically generating verifiable sheet music problems provides a practical and effective approach for strengthening reasoning capabilities in sheet music comprehension.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Evaluation results on the MusicTheoryBench. Training the Qwen3-8B-Base model with GRPO and our synthetic data leads to substantial gains in both knowledge and reasoning. Notably, our enhanced model also outperforms the Qwen3-8B, especially in the reasoning category.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center">Models</span></th>
