<td id="S5.T5.2.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:0.0pt;padding-right:0.0pt;"><math id="S5.T5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="79.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>79.40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">79.40</annotation></semantics></math><sup id="S5.T5.2.1.6.4.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T5.2.1.6.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9D595C;">(+13.40)</span></sup></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:0.0pt;padding-right:0.0pt;"><math id="S5.T5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="34.19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>34.19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">34.19</annotation></semantics></math><sup id="S5.T5.2.1.6.5.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T5.2.1.6.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9D595C;">(+8.82)</span></sup></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:0.0pt;padding-right:0.0pt;"><math id="S5.T5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40.44" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40.44</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40.44</annotation></semantics></math><sup id="S5.T5.2.1.6.6.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T5.2.1.6.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9D595C;">(+10.22)</span></sup></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>From Question Answering to Sheet Music Generation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate whether improved sheet music reasoning can facilitate music composition, we extend our evaluation from question answering to sheet music generation. Specifically, we prompt models to generate a four-measure continuation conditioned on an initial four-measure excerpt of sheet music in ABC notation. We evaluate these continuations on rhythmic consistency, a dimension that prior studies have identified as particularly challenging for sheet music generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suzuki, 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce two primary evaluation metrics:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rhythmic Consistency (RC)</span>: The average of a binary score assigned to each generated continuation. A sample is scored 1 if all four measures are rhythmically correct and 0 otherwise.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Novel Rhythmic Coherence (NRC)</span>: While melody repetition is common in music, we also aim to assess a model’s likelihood of producing rhythmically correct sheet music when generating novel melodies. The NRC metric is designed for this purpose. It is calculated as the RC score, but only for generated continuations that are not direct copies of the provided prompt.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.04059v1/generation.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/170;" width="274" height="170"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison of rhythmic consistency.</span> The figure shows the Rhythmic Consistency (RC) and Novel Rhythmic Coherence (NRC) for Qwen3-8B-Base and its GRPO variant, trained on our synthetic data.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We randomly sample 30 pieces of sheet music from SSMR-bench and evaluate each model’s continuation through human judgment. The results in Table <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2 From Question Answering to Sheet Music Generation ‣ 5 Beyond Synthetic Data and Question Answering Tasks ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> show that training on synthetic sheet music problems also leads to improvements in music composition. The base Qwen3-8B model achieves an RC score of 66.67%. After reinforcement learning on our synthetic dataset, this score dramatically increases to 99.33%, surpassing the 70.00% from the original Qwen3-8B. The improvement in generating novel content is also evident: our fine-tuned model’s NRC score of 88.89% is a significant increase over the base model’s 50.00% and the original Qwen3-8B’s 68.97%.
Although we focus only on the rhythm dimension, which is just one aspect of music, the observed improvements also highlight the potential for enhancing reasoning abilities to facilitate music composition.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Possible Challenges Facing AI Musicians.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The findings presented in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Results on Synthetic Music Reasoning Benchmark ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Synthesizing Sheet Music Problems for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> highlight the varying levels of difficulty associated with different modalities of sheet music. In particular, the same question, when presented in visual sheet music format, is significantly more challenging for LLMs than when conveyed in a textual format. Beyond modality, sheet music can be represented in a variety of formats, including MIDI, MusicXML, and others. Furthermore, sheet music is not limited to traditional staff notation; alternative formats, such as Guitar Tablature and numbered musical notation, should also be considered, which together present substantial challenges for the development of an ideal AI musician.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations and Future Work.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we focus on synthesizing verifiable sheet music reasoning problems based on widely accepted rules in music theory. We implement this approach through a data generation framework and evaluate its effectiveness. However, the questions we construct are relatively simple, and can be reliably solved by Deepseek-R1. At the same time, they highlight the challenge for MLLMs to fully understand music sheets, as evidenced by the low performance of Gemini2.5-Pro. Taken together, these observations motivate future work on designing more complex, rule-based, verifiable music reasoning questions and on developing improved methods to enhance models’ reasoning abilities of sheet music, as well as transferring these abilities to music composition.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce the concept of synthesizing sheet music problems based on the rules of music theory, enabling the construction of verifiable questions for evaluation and training. We implement this idea by proposing a framework that automatically generates sheet music questions with staff notation in both textual and visual modalities. Based on this framework, we introduce SSMR-Bench and a corresponding training set in both modalities.
Experimental results demonstrate that the synthesized data effectively enhances the reasoning capabilities of LLMs and MLLMs in sheet music tasks, underscoring its potential as a valuable resource.
Moreover, the results also highlight the promise of this enhanced ability to facilitate music composition.
Our work demonstrates the potential of programmatically generating verifiable sheet music problems to evaluate and improve the abilities of LLMs and MLLMs in sheet music, while also highlighting promising avenues for future research toward AI musicianship.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
